.TH DEMAKE\-RENDER 1 "" "" "Render a compliant audio artifact to a playable audio file"
.SH NAME
demake-render \- Render a compliant audio artifact to a playable audio file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdemake render\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIinput\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Render a compliant audio artifact to a playable audio file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-output-stage\fR \fIvalue\fR
Raw chip output, or the console's analog stage simulated. (default: raw)
.TP
\fB\-\-sample-rate\fR \fI<hz>\fR
Delivery rate; 48000 unless you have a reason. (default: 48000)
.TP
\fB\-\-loops\fR \fI<n>\fR
Repeat from the loop point this many extra times. (default: 0)
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<file>\fR
Write the artifact to <file> (default: stdout when piped).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single JSON object on stdout (product goes to \-o).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic detail (repeatable).
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress warnings.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite an existing output file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
demake render song.vgm \-o song.wav
.br
hear it as the hardware plays it
.PP
demake render song.vgm \-\-loops 2 \-o song.wav
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBdemake\fR(1)
